00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:11.772
Problem.

6
00:00:13.022 --> 00:00:26.462
Answer has this username or URL or key been seen? for 100k (or 1 billion) items in a fraction of the memory a set would use, with no false negatives and about 1% false positives.

7
00:00:26.742 --> 00:00:30.326
Size it from the target error rate and verify it empirically.

8
00:00:31.676 --> 00:00:32.551
Part 2.

9
00:00:32.831 --> 00:00:33.748
Key idea.

10
00:00:34.998 --> 00:00:43.723
Use m bits and k hash functions. add sets k bits; mightContain checks them, and if any is 0 the item is definitely absent.

11
00:00:44.003 --> 00:00:57.806
For n items and a target rate p: m = minus n, ln p / (ln 2)², k = (m or n), ln 2, which is about 9.6 bits and 7 hashes per item for 1%.

12
00:00:58.086 --> 00:01:03.419
Two base hashes simulate all k (h1 + i, h2, Kirsch, Mitzenmacher).

13
00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:12.787
It's used in front of databases (Cassandra, HBase, RocksDB SSTables) to skip disk reads for keys that are certainly absent.

14
