110
00:08:10.163 --> 00:08:11.037
Part 8.

111
00:08:11.317 --> 00:08:12.427
Common mistakes.

112
00:08:13.677 --> 00:08:15.234
Expecting deletes to work.

113
00:08:15.514 --> 00:08:17.626
Clearing a bit can remove other items.

114
00:08:17.906 --> 00:08:20.573
Use a Counting Bloom filter or a Cuckoo filter.

115
00:08:20.853 --> 00:08:21.877
Under-sizing.

116
00:08:22.157 --> 00:08:25.826
Inserting more than n items makes the FPR grow fast.

117
00:08:26.106 --> 00:08:28.645
Rebuild, or use a scalable Bloom filter.

118
00:08:28.925 --> 00:08:32.103
Using String.hashCode() with k simple variants.

119
00:08:32.383 --> 00:08:34.773
Correlated hashes raise the FPR.

120
00:08:36.123 --> 00:08:37.019
Part 9.

121
00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:38.643
Follow-up questions.

122
00:08:39.893 --> 00:08:40.767
Question.

123
00:08:41.047 --> 00:08:43.330
Where does a Bloom filter sit in a read path?

124
00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:44.975
Thirty-second answer:

125
00:08:45.255 --> 00:08:59.847
In front of an expensive lookup that usually misses: per-SSTable filters in LSM databases, username taken? checks, crawler URL already seen? checks, CDN is this object cached anywhere? checks.

126
00:09:00.127 --> 00:09:02.922
A negative answer skips the I or O entirely.

127
00:09:04.272 --> 00:09:05.147
Question.

128
00:09:05.427 --> 00:09:07.048
Bloom versus Cuckoo filter?

129
00:09:07.328 --> 00:09:08.693
Thirty-second answer:

130
00:09:08.973 --> 00:09:16.739
Cuckoo filters support delete, have better lookup locality, and use less space at low FPRs (below about 3%).

131
00:09:17.019 --> 00:09:19.728
Inserts can fail when the table is nearly full.

132
00:09:20.008 --> 00:09:23.144
Bloom filters are simpler and their inserts never fail.

